1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,075
Chapter 10: Prenatal Care.

3
00:00:05,075 --> 00:00:36,100
Prenatal Care In The United States Diagnosis Of Pregnancy Initial Prenatal Evaluation Subsequent Prenatal Visits Nutritional Counseling Common Concerns References The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) define prenatal care as “A comprehensive antepartum program involves a coordinated approach to medical care, continuous risk assessment, and psychosocial support that optimally begins before pregnancy and extends throughout the

4
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:39,187
postpartum and interpregnancy period.

5
00:00:39,187 --> 00:00:47,225
As promulgated by John Ballantyne, such care has been a bedrock to improve pregnancy outcomes for more than 100 years .

6
00:00:47,225 --> 00:00:50,187
Prenatal Care In The United States.

7
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:58,612
Almost a century after its introduction, prenatal care has become one of the most frequently used health services in the United States.

8
00:00:58,612 --> 00:01:09,262
According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), only 1.6 percent of women who gave birth in 2016 received no prenatal care .

9
00:01:09,262 --> 00:01:18,412
African-American and Hispanic women have high rates of inadequate or no prenatal care that reach 10 and 7.7 percent, respectively.

10
00:01:18,412 --> 00:01:26,037
This figure is greater for adolescents, particularly those younger than 15 years, compared with older age groups.

11
00:01:26,037 --> 00:01:30,962
These data highlight areas of potential improvement by the health-care system.

12
00:01:30,962 --> 00:01:34,112
# Prenatal Care Effectiveness.

13
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:40,875
Care designed during the early 1900s focused on lowering the extremely high maternal mortality rate.

14
00:01:40,875 --> 00:01:53,050
Prenatal care undoubtedly contributed to the dramatic decline in maternal deaths from 690 per 100,000 births in 1920 to 50 per 100,000 by 1955 .

15
00:01:53,050 --> 00:02:04,362
Data from 1998 to 2005 from the Pregnancy Mortality Surveillance System identified a fivefold increased risk for maternal death in women who received no prenatal care .

16
00:02:04,362 --> 00:02:12,412
Goldenberg and McClure (2018) have emphasized the importance of prenatal care to reduce stillbirth rates as well.

17
00:02:12,412 --> 00:02:24,762
In a study of almost 29 million births, the risk for preterm birth, stillbirth, early and late neonatal death, and infant death rose linearly with decreasing prenatal care utilization .

18
00:02:24,762 --> 00:02:38,012
Similarly, from Parkland Hospital, Leveno and associates (2009) found that a significant decline in preterm births correlated closely with any use of prenatal care by medically indigent women.

19
00:02:38,012 --> 00:02:46,387
And in women with diabetes, adherence to prenatal care resulted in lower rates of neonatal admissions to the intensive care unit .

20
00:02:46,387 --> 00:02:49,925
Group prenatal care is acceptable and effective .

21
00:02:49,925 --> 00:02:56,300
Ickovics and coworkers (2016) compared this with individual prenatal care.

22
00:02:56,300 --> 00:03:05,487
Group care provided traditional pregnancy surveillance in a group setting with special focus on support, education, and active health-care participation.

23
00:03:05,487 --> 00:03:10,362
Women enrolled in group care had significantly better pregnancy outcomes.

24
00:03:10,362 --> 00:03:15,300
Carter and colleagues (2017) cited similar results.

25
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:21,262
Childbirth education classes are also reported to result in better pregnancy outcomes .

26
00:03:21,262 --> 00:03:28,312
Pregnancy in adolescents carries special risk, and guidelines have been developed that focus on this age group .

27
00:03:28,312 --> 00:03:30,775
Diagnosis Of Pregnancy.

28
00:03:30,775 --> 00:03:38,675
Pregnancy is usually identified when a woman presents with symptoms and possibly a positive home urine pregnancy test result.

29
00:03:38,675 --> 00:03:46,625
Typically, these women receive confirmatory testing of urine or blood for human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG).

30
00:03:46,625 --> 00:03:54,075
Further, presumptive signs or diagnostic findings of pregnancy may be found during the clinical examination.

31
00:03:54,075 --> 00:04:00,337
Sonography is often used, especially if miscarriage or ectopic pregnancy is a concern.

32
00:04:00,337 --> 00:04:03,075
# Symptoms and Signs.

33
00:04:03,075 --> 00:04:13,237
Amenorrhea in a healthy reproductive-aged woman who previously has experienced spontaneous, cyclical, predictable menses is highly suggestive of pregnancy.

34
00:04:13,237 --> 00:04:18,725
Menstrual cycles vary appreciably in length among women and even in the same woman .

35
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:26,550
Thus, amenorrhea is not a reliable pregnancy indicator until 10 or more days have passed after expected menses.

36
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:32,200
Occasionally, uterine bleeding that mimics menstruation is noted after conception.

37
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:39,025
During the first month of pregnancy, these episodes are likely the consequence of blastocyst implantation.

38
00:04:39,025 --> 00:04:42,550
Still, first-trimester bleeding should prompt

39
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:46,112
evaluation for an abnormal pregnancy.

40
00:04:46,062 --> 00:04:52,850
Of other symptoms, maternal perception of fetal movement depends on factors such as parity and habitus.

41
00:04:52,850 --> 00:05:01,475
In general, after a first successful pregnancy, a woman may first perceive fetal movements between 16 and 18 weeks’ gestation.

42
00:05:01,475 --> 00:05:07,200
A primigravida may not appreciate fetal movements until approximately 2 weeks later.

43
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:14,162
At about 20 weeks, depending on maternal habitus, an examiner can begin to detect fetal movements.

44
00:05:14,162 --> 00:05:20,900
Of pregnancy signs, changes in the lower reproductive tract, uterus, and breasts develop early.

45
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:23,512
# Pregnancy Tests.

46
00:05:23,512 --> 00:05:29,937
Detection of hCG in maternal blood and urine is the basis for endocrine assays of pregnancy.

47
00:05:29,937 --> 00:05:37,075
Syncytiotrophoblast produces hCG in amounts that increase exponentially during the first trimester.

48
00:05:37,075 --> 00:05:42,862
hCG and luteinizing hormone (LH) share the same receptor in tissues.

49
00:05:42,862 --> 00:05:53,700
Thus, a main function of hCG is to prevent involution of the corpus luteum, which is the principal site of progesterone formation during the first 6 weeks of pregnancy.

50
00:05:53,700 --> 00:06:01,200
With a sensitive test, the hormone can be detected in maternal serum or urine by 8 to 9 days after ovulation.

51
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:07,400
The doubling time of serum hCG concentration is 1.4 to 2.0 days.

52
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:14,262
As shown in Figure 10-1, serum levels range widely and increase from the day of implantation.

53
00:06:14,262 --> 00:06:18,875
Lower levels of hCG rise more rapidly than higher levels .

54
00:06:18,875 --> 00:06:23,250
Peak hCG levels are reached at 60 to 70 days.

55
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:30,462
Thereafter, the concentration declines slowly to a plateau at approximately 16 weeks’ gestation.

56
00:06:30,462 --> 00:06:32,625
Figure 10-1.

57
00:06:32,625 --> 00:06:42,362
Mean concentration (95% CI) of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) in serum of women throughout normal pregnancy.

58
00:06:42,362 --> 00:06:48,125
Measurement of hCG This hormone is a glycoprotein with high carbohydrate content.

59
00:06:48,125 --> 00:06:57,762
The general structure of hCG is a heterodimer composed of two dissimilar subunits, designated α and β, which are noncovalently linked.

60
00:06:57,762 --> 00:07:09,625
The α-subunit is identical to those of LH, follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), and thyroidstimulating hormone (TSH), but the β-subunit is structurally distinct.

61
00:07:09,625 --> 00:07:15,525
Thus, antibodies were developed with high specificity for the hCG β-subunit.

62
00:07:15,525 --> 00:07:24,662
This specificity allows its detection, and numerous commercial immunoassays are available for measuring serum and urine hCG levels.

63
00:07:24,662 --> 00:07:35,562
Each immunoassay detects a slightly different mixture of hCG variants, its free subunits, or its metabolites—however, all are appropriate for pregnancy testing .

64
00:07:35,562 --> 00:07:45,562
Depending on the assay used, the sensitivity for the laboratory detection limit of hCG in serum is 1.0 mIU/mL or even lower.

65
00:07:45,562 --> 00:07:49,287
False-positive hCG test results are rare.

66
00:07:49,287 --> 00:07:57,825
A few women have circulating serum factors that may bind erroneously with the test antibody directed to hCG in a given assay.

67
00:07:57,825 --> 00:08:01,712
The most common factors are heterophilic antibodies.

68
00:08:01,712 --> 00:08:08,812
These are produced by an individual and bind to the animal-derived test antibodies used in a given immunoassay.

69
00:08:08,812 --> 00:08:17,762
Thus, women who have worked closely with animals are more likely to develop these antibodies, and alternative laboratory techniques are available .

70
00:08:17,762 --> 00:08:24,275
Elevated hCG levels may also reflect molar pregnancy and its associated neoplasms .

71
00:08:24,275 --> 00:08:47,212
Other rare causes of positive assays without pregnancy are (1) exogenous hCG injection used for weight loss, (2) renal failure with impaired hCG clearance, (3) physiological pituitary hCG, and (4) hCG-producing tumors that most commonly originate from gastrointestinal sites, ovary, bladder, or lung .

72
00:08:47,212 --> 00:08:54,975
Home Pregnancy Tests More than 60 different types of over-the-counter pregnancy test kits are available in the United States .

73
00:08:54,975 --> 00:08:59,537
Unfortunately, many of these are not as accurate as advertised .

74
00:08:59,537 --> 00:09:12,787
For example, Cole and associates (2011) found that a detection limit of 12.5 mIU/mL would be required to diagnose 95 percent of pregnancies at the time of missed menses.

75
00:09:12,787 --> 00:09:17,912
However, they reported that only one brand had this degree of sensitivity.

76
00:09:17,912 --> 00:09:22,137
Two other brands gave false-positive or invalid results.

77
00:09:22,137 --> 00:09:32,000
In fact, with an hCG concentration of 100 mIU/mL, clearly positive results were displayed by only 44 percent of brands.

78
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:39,175
Accordingly, only approximately 15 percent of pregnancies could be diagnosed at the time of the missed menses.

79
00:09:39,175 --> 00:09:41,650
Some manufacturers of

80
00:09:41,812 --> 00:09:51,687
even newer home urine assays claim >99-percent accuracy for tests done on the day of—and some up to 4 days before—the expected day of menses.

81
00:09:51,637 --> 00:09:58,087
Again, careful analysis suggests that these assays are often not as sensitive as advertised.

82
00:09:58,087 --> 00:10:01,824
# Sonographic Recognition of Pregnancy.

83
00:10:01,824 --> 00:10:09,462
Transvaginal sonography is commonly used to accurately establish gestational age and confirm pregnancy location.

84
00:10:09,462 --> 00:10:19,049
A gestational sac is the first sonographic evidence of pregnancy, and it may be seen with transvaginal sonography by 4 to 5 weeks’ gestation.

85
00:10:19,049 --> 00:10:22,912
It should not be confused with a pseudogestational sac.

86
00:10:22,912 --> 00:10:34,099
The latter, or pseudosac, is a fluid collection within the endometrial cavity, which can occur in the setting of ectopic pregnancy (Fig. 12-3, p. 223).

87
00:10:34,099 --> 00:10:42,074
Further evaluation may be warranted if this is the only sonographic finding, particularly in a woman with pain or bleeding.

88
00:10:42,074 --> 00:10:50,524
A normal gestational sac implants eccentrically in the endometrium, whereas a pseudosac is seen in the midline of the endometrial cavity.

89
00:10:50,524 --> 00:11:05,662
Other potential indicators of early intrauterine pregnancy are an anechoic center surrounded by a single echogenic rim—the intradecidual sign—or two concentric echogenic rings surrounding the gestational sac—the double decidual sign .

90
00:11:05,662 --> 00:11:12,937
If sonography yields equivocal findings, the term pregnancy of unknown location (PUL) is applied .

91
00:11:12,937 --> 00:11:24,949
In these cases, serial serum hCG levels and transvaginal sonography can help differentiate a normal intrauterine pregnancy from an extrauterine pregnancy or an early miscarriage .

92
00:11:24,949 --> 00:11:27,187
Figure 10-2.

93
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:32,212
Transvaginal sonogram of a first-trimester intrauterine pregnancy.

94
00:11:32,212 --> 00:11:43,312
The double decidual sign is noted surrounding the gestational sac and is defined by the decidua parietalis (white asterisk) and the decidua capsularis (yellow asterisk).

95
00:11:43,312 --> 00:11:49,824
The arrow notes the yolk sac, and the crown-rump length of the embryo is marked with measuring calipers.

96
00:11:49,824 --> 00:11:53,824
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Elysia Moschos.

97
00:11:53,824 --> 00:12:04,274
If the yolk sac—a brightly echogenic ring with an anechoic center—is seen within the gestational sac, an intrauterine location for the pregnancy is confirmed.

98
00:12:04,274 --> 00:12:08,562
The yolk sac can normally be seen by the middle of the fifth week.

99
00:12:08,562 --> 00:12:17,099
As shown in Figure 10-2, after 6 weeks, an embryo is seen as a linear structure immediately adjacent to the yolk sac.

100
00:12:17,099 --> 00:12:20,612
Cardiac motion is typically noted at this point.

101
00:12:20,612 --> 00:12:23,399
Initial Prenatal Evaluation.

102
00:12:23,399 --> 00:12:26,862
Prenatal care is ideally initiated early.

103
00:12:26,862 --> 00:12:38,249
Major goals are to (1) define the health status of the mother and fetus, (2) estimate the gestational age, and (3) initiate a plan for continued obstetrical care.

104
00:12:38,249 --> 00:12:43,387
Typical components of the initial visit are summarized in Table 10-1.

105
00:12:43,387 --> 00:12:51,949
Subsequent care may range from relatively infrequent routine visits to prompt hospitalization because of serious maternal or fetal disease.

106
00:12:51,949 --> 00:12:54,099
Table 10-1.

107
00:12:54,099 --> 00:12:59,012
Typical Components of Routine Prenatal Care # Prenatal Record.

108
00:12:59,012 --> 00:13:06,362
Use of a standardized record within a perinatal health-care system greatly aids antepartum and intrapartum management.

109
00:13:06,362 --> 00:13:18,537
Standardizing documentation allows communication and care continuity between providers and enables objective measures of care quality to be evaluated over time and across different clinical settings .

110
00:13:18,537 --> 00:13:31,149
A prototype is provided by the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) in their Guidelines for Perinatal Care, 8th edition.

111
00:13:31,149 --> 00:13:37,162
Definitions Several definitions are pertinent to establishment of an accurate prenatal record.

112
00:13:37,162 --> 00:13:42,987
1. Nulligravida—a woman who currently is not pregnant and has never been pregnant.

113
00:13:42,987 --> 00:13:50,149
2. Gravida—a woman who currently is pregnant or has been in the past, irrespective of the pregnancy outcome.

114
00:13:50,149 --> 00:13:57,987
With the establishment of the first pregnancy, she becomes a primigravida, and with successive pregnancies, a multigravida.

115
00:13:57,987 --> 00:14:04,137
3. Nullipara—a woman who has never completed a pregnancy beyond 20 weeks’ gestation.

116
00:14:04,137 --> 00:14:10,912
She may not have been pregnant or may have had a spontaneous or elective abortion(s) or an ectopic pregnancy.

117
00:14:10,912 --> 00:14:19,074
4. Primipara—a woman who has been delivered only once of a fetus or fetuses born alive or dead with an estimated gestation

118
00:14:19,228 --> 00:14:22,040
duration of 20 or more weeks.

119
00:14:21,990 --> 00:14:27,365
In the past, a 500-g birthweight threshold was used to define parity.

120
00:14:27,365 --> 00:14:30,303
This threshold is now controversial.

121
00:14:30,303 --> 00:14:41,865
Namely, many states still use this weight to differentiate a stillborn fetus from an abortus, but the survival of neonates with birthweights <500 g is no longer uncommon .

122
00:14:41,865 --> 00:14:49,440
5. Multipara—a woman who has completed two or more pregnancies with gestational ages at least 20 weeks.

123
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:54,053
Parity is determined by the number of pregnancies reaching 20 weeks.

124
00:14:54,053 --> 00:14:59,390
It is not increased to a higher number if multiples are delivered in a given pregnancy.

125
00:14:59,390 --> 00:15:02,965
Moreover, stillbirth does not lower this number.

126
00:15:02,965 --> 00:15:09,690
In some locales, the obstetrical history is summarized by a series of digits connected by dashes.

127
00:15:09,690 --> 00:15:18,128
These refer to the number of term newborns, preterm neonates, abortuses younger than 20 weeks, and children currently alive.

128
00:15:18,128 --> 00:15:28,578
For example, a woman who is para 2–1–0–3 has had two term deliveries, one preterm delivery, no abortuses, and has three living children.

129
00:15:28,578 --> 00:15:35,640
Because these are nonconventional, it is helpful to specify the outcome of any pregnancy that did not end normally.

130
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:46,265
Normal Pregnancy Duration The normal duration of pregnancy calculated from the first day of the last normal menstrual period is very close to 280 days or 40 weeks.

131
00:15:46,265 --> 00:15:56,253
A quick estimate of a pregnancy due date based on menstrual data can be made as follows: add 7 days to the first day of the last period and subtract 3 months.

132
00:15:56,253 --> 00:16:09,453
For example, if the first day of the last menses was October 5, the due date is 10-05 minus 3 (months) plus 7 (days) = 7–12 or July 12 of the following year.

133
00:16:09,453 --> 00:16:12,340
This calculation is the Naegele rule.

134
00:16:12,340 --> 00:16:19,190
However, menstrual cycle length varies among women and renders many of these calculations inaccurate.

135
00:16:19,190 --> 00:16:27,678
This, combined with the frequent use of first-trimester sonography, has changed the method of determining an accurate gestational age.

136
00:16:27,678 --> 00:16:45,453
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017e), the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine, and the Society for MaternalFetal Medicine have emphasized that first-trimester ultrasound is the most accurate method to establish or reaffirm gestational age.

137
00:16:45,453 --> 00:16:54,103
For pregnancies conceived by assisted reproductive technologies, embryo age or transfer date is used to assign gestational age.

138
00:16:54,103 --> 00:17:05,115
If available, the gestational ages calculated from the last menstrual period and from first-trimester ultrasound are compared, and this estimated date of delivery is recorded.

139
00:17:05,115 --> 00:17:12,528
Reconciling any discordance between these two values is discussed in Chapter 14 (p. 248).

140
00:17:12,528 --> 00:17:21,078
Trimesters It has become customary to divide pregnancy into three equal epochs or trimesters of approximately 3 calendar months.

141
00:17:21,078 --> 00:17:28,140
More recently a “fourth trimester” has been recognized to emphasize the need for comprehensive postpartum care .

142
00:17:28,140 --> 00:17:32,803
This is discussed in Chapter 36 (p. 634).

143
00:17:32,803 --> 00:17:41,803
Historically, the first trimester extends through completion of 14 weeks, the second through 28 weeks, and the third through 42 weeks.

144
00:17:41,803 --> 00:17:44,990
The fourth is the 12 weeks after delivery.

145
00:17:44,990 --> 00:17:49,640
Thus, prenatally, there are three periods of 14 weeks each.

146
00:17:49,640 --> 00:17:55,128
Certain major obstetrical problems tend to cluster in each of these three time periods.

147
00:17:55,128 --> 00:18:06,440
For example, most spontaneous abortions take place during the first trimester, whereas most women with hypertensive disorders due to pregnancy are diagnosed during the third trimester.

148
00:18:06,440 --> 00:18:13,428
In modern obstetrics, the clinical use of trimesters to describe a specific pregnancy is imprecise.

149
00:18:13,428 --> 00:18:21,478
For example, it is inappropriate in cases of uterine hemorrhage to categorize the problem temporally as “third-trimester bleeding.

150
00:18:21,478 --> 00:18:29,890
Appropriate management for the mother and her fetus will vary remarkably depending on whether bleeding begins early or late in the third trimester .

151
00:18:29,890 --> 00:18:38,615
Because precise knowledge of fetal age is imperative for obstetrical management, the clinically appropriate unit is weeks of gestation completed.

152
00:18:38,615 --> 00:18:43,503
Clinicians designate gestational age using completed weeks and days.

153
00:18:43,503 --> 00:18:53,128
For example, 334/7 weeks or 33 + 4 describes pregnancy duration of 33 completed weeks and 4 days.

154
00:18:53,128 --> 00:18:57,040
Previous and Current Health Status As elsewhere in medicine,

155
00:18:57,196 --> 00:19:02,371
history-taking begins with queries concerning medical or surgical disorders.

156
00:19:02,321 --> 00:19:11,371
Detailed information regarding previous pregnancies is essential, as many obstetrical complications tend to recur in subsequent pregnancies.

157
00:19:11,371 --> 00:19:15,558
The menstrual and contraceptive histories also are important.

158
00:19:15,558 --> 00:19:21,721
As noted earlier, gestational age may be less accurate for those with irregular menses.

159
00:19:21,721 --> 00:19:28,058
Moreover, some methods of birth control favor ectopic implantation following method failure .

160
00:19:28,058 --> 00:19:30,296
Psychosocial Screening.

161
00:19:30,296 --> 00:19:43,983
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) define psychosocial issues as nonbiomedical factors that affect mental and physical well-being.

162
00:19:43,983 --> 00:19:50,396
Women should be screened regardless of social status, education level, race, or ethnicity.

163
00:19:50,396 --> 00:20:08,508
Such screening should seek barriers to care, communication obstacles, nutritional status, unstable housing, desire for pregnancy, safety concerns that include intimate-partner violence, depression, stress, and use of substances such as tobacco, alcohol, and illicit drugs.

164
00:20:08,508 --> 00:20:18,171
This screening is performed on a regular basis, at least once per trimester, to identify important issues and reduce adverse pregnancy outcomes.

165
00:20:18,171 --> 00:20:34,608
Coker and colleagues (2012) compared pregnancy outcomes in women before and after implementation of a universal psychosocial screening program and found that screened women were less likely to have preterm or low-birthweight newborns, as well as other adverse outcomes.

166
00:20:34,608 --> 00:20:40,971
Specific screens for depression are presented in Chapter 64 (p. 1143).

167
00:20:40,971 --> 00:20:43,033
Cigarette Smoking.

168
00:20:43,033 --> 00:20:49,933
These data are included on the birth certificate, and the number of pregnant women who smoke continues to decline.

169
00:20:49,933 --> 00:20:55,571
From 2000 to 2010, the prevalences were 12 to 13 percent .

170
00:20:55,571 --> 00:21:02,933
By 2016, the incidence was 7.2 percent according to the National Center for Health Statistics .

171
00:21:02,933 --> 00:21:13,308
Concurrent with the decline in cigarette use, there has been an increase in electronic cigarettes/vaping with a reported prevalence of 0.6 to 15 percent .

172
00:21:13,308 --> 00:21:24,133
In a survey of more than 3000 mothers in Oklahoma and Texas, 7 percent reported using electronic vapor products prior to conception and in the postpartum period.

173
00:21:24,133 --> 00:21:31,321
Of these women, according to the CDC, 1.4 percent used them during the last 3 months of pregnancy .

174
00:21:31,321 --> 00:21:39,321
According to the American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2018), smoking is associated with subfertility.

175
00:21:39,321 --> 00:21:52,858
Higher rates of miscarriage, stillbirth, low birthweight, and preterm delivery also are linked to smoking during pregnancy (Dahlin, 2016; Luke, 2018; Tong, 2013).

176
00:21:52,858 --> 00:22:01,508
Compared with nonsmokers, risks of placenta previa, placental abruption, and premature membrane rupture are increased twofold.

177
00:22:01,508 --> 00:22:07,333
Potential teratogenic effects are reviewed in Chapter 8 (p. 156).

178
00:22:07,333 --> 00:22:18,771
Thus, the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force recommends that clinicians offer counseling and effective intervention options to pregnant smokers at the first and subsequent prenatal visits .

179
00:22:18,771 --> 00:22:29,196
Although benefits are greatest if smoking ceases early in pregnancy or preferably preconceptionally, quitting at any stage of pregnancy can improve perinatal outcomes .

180
00:22:29,196 --> 00:22:39,283
Compared with simple counseling to quit, person-to-person psychosocial interventions are significantly more successful in achieving smoking abstinence in pregnancy .

181
00:22:39,283 --> 00:22:44,758
One example is a brief counseling session covering the “5As” of smoking cessation .

182
00:22:44,758 --> 00:22:51,683
This approach can be accomplished in 15 minutes or less and is effective when initiated by health-care providers .

183
00:22:51,683 --> 00:22:53,996
Table 10-2.

184
00:22:53,996 --> 00:23:02,471
Five A’s of Smoking Cessation Behavioral interventions and nicotine replacement products are successful in reducing smoking rates .

185
00:23:02,471 --> 00:23:10,183
However, nicotine replacement has not been sufficiently evaluated to determine its effectiveness and safety in pregnancy.

186
00:23:10,183 --> 00:23:19,008
Trials evaluating such therapy have yielded conflicting evidence (Coleman, 2015; Spindel, 2016).

187
00:23:19,008 --> 00:23:23,533
Two randomized trials also produced inconclusive results.

188
00:23:23,533 --> 00:23:36,208
In the Smoking and Nicotine in Pregnancy (SNAP) trial, Cooper and associates (2014) reported that a temporary cessation of smoking may have been associated with improved infant development.

189
00:23:36,208 --> 00:23:39,633
In the Study of Nicotine Patch in Pregnancy

190
00:23:39,796 --> 00:23:45,571
SNIPP) trial, no differences in smoking cessation rates or birthweights were found .

191
00:23:45,521 --> 00:23:50,558
Similar preliminary results were reported for sustainedrelease bupropion .

192
00:23:50,558 --> 00:23:58,196
Olson and colleagues (2019) reported that financial incentives were helpful to encourage smoking cessation.

193
00:23:58,196 --> 00:24:19,371
Because of limited available evidence to support pharmacotherapy for smoking cessation in pregnancy, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) recommends that if nicotine replacement therapy is used, it should be done with close supervision and after careful consideration of the risks of smoking versus nicotine replacement.

194
00:24:19,371 --> 00:24:20,971
Alcohol.

195
00:24:20,971 --> 00:24:27,321
Ethyl alcohol or ethanol is a potent teratogen that causes the fetal alcohol spectrum disorders.

196
00:24:27,321 --> 00:24:38,171
Fetal alcohol syndrome, the most severe form of these disorders, is characterized by growth restriction, facial abnormalities, and central nervous system dysfunction.

197
00:24:38,171 --> 00:24:43,371
The estimated prevalence of these disorders is 11 to 50 per 1000 .

198
00:24:43,371 --> 00:24:52,521
As discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 150), women who are pregnant or considering pregnancy should abstain from drinking any alcoholic beverages .

199
00:24:52,521 --> 00:25:03,721
The CDC analyzed data from the Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System from 2015 to 2017 and estimated that 12 percent of pregnant women used alcohol .

200
00:25:03,721 --> 00:25:23,621
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021a) in collaboration with the CDC has developed the Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorders (FASD) Prevention Program, which provides resources for providers and is available at www.acog.org/alcohol.

201
00:25:23,621 --> 00:25:25,558
Illicit Drugs.

202
00:25:25,558 --> 00:25:30,971
An estimated 10 percent of fetuses are exposed to one or more illicit drugs.

203
00:25:30,971 --> 00:25:38,071
Agents may include heroin and other opiates, cocaine, amphetamines, barbiturates, and marijuana .

204
00:25:38,071 --> 00:25:45,621
As discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 157), chronic use of large quantities is harmful to the fetus .

205
00:25:45,621 --> 00:25:52,933
Well-documented sequelae include fetal-growth restriction, low birthweight, and drug withdrawal soon after birth.

206
00:25:52,933 --> 00:25:56,633
Adverse effects of marijuana are less convincing.

207
00:25:56,633 --> 00:26:05,808
Women who use such drugs frequently do not seek prenatal care, which in itself is associated with risks for preterm and low-birthweight neonates .

208
00:26:05,808 --> 00:26:21,021
For women who abuse heroin, methadone maintenance can be initiated within a registered methadone treatment program to reduce complications of illicit opioid use and narcotic withdrawal, to encourage prenatal care, and to avoid drug culture risks .

209
00:26:21,021 --> 00:26:31,521
Available programs can be found through the treatment locator of the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration at www.samhsa.gov.

210
00:26:31,521 --> 00:26:38,658
Methadone dosages usually are initiated at 10 to 30 mg orally daily and titrated as needed.

211
00:26:38,658 --> 00:26:43,471
In some women, careful methadone taper may be an appropriate option .

212
00:26:43,471 --> 00:26:51,221
Buprenorphine alone or in combination with naloxone also may be offered and managed by physicians with specific credentialing.

213
00:26:51,221 --> 00:26:58,058
These therapeutic options are considered in greater detail in Chapter 64 (p. 1150).

214
00:26:58,058 --> 00:27:00,483
Intimate-Partner Violence.

215
00:27:00,483 --> 00:27:14,908
This term refers to a pattern of assault and coercive behavior that may include physical injury, psychological abuse, sexual assault, progressive isolation, stalking, deprivation, intimidation, and reproductive coercion .

216
00:27:14,908 --> 00:27:19,196
Such violence is recognized as a major public health problem.

217
00:27:19,196 --> 00:27:24,658
Unfortunately, most abused women continue to be victimized during pregnancy.

218
00:27:24,658 --> 00:27:35,596
With the possible exception of preeclampsia, intimate-partner violence (IPV) is more prevalent than any major medical condition detectable through routine prenatal screening .

219
00:27:35,596 --> 00:27:40,608
The estimated prevalence during pregnancy lies between 4 and 8 percent.

220
00:27:40,608 --> 00:27:50,821
IPV is associated with an increased risk of several adverse perinatal outcomes that include preterm delivery, fetal-growth restriction, and perinatal death .

221
00:27:50,821 --> 00:28:05,996
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c) has provided methods for IPV screening and recommends their use at the first prenatal visit, again at least once per trimester, and again at the postpartum visit.

222
00:28:05,996 --> 00:28:11,058
Such screening should be done privately and away from family members and friends.

223
00:28:11,058 --> 00:28:18,258
Patient self-administered or computerized screenings appear to be as effective for disclosure as clinician-directed

224
00:28:18,412 --> 00:28:23,424
interviews (Ahmad, 2009; Chen, 2007).

225
00:28:23,374 --> 00:28:28,849
Physicians should be familiar with state laws that may require reporting of IPV.

226
00:28:28,849 --> 00:28:33,562
Coordination with social services can be invaluable in these cases.

227
00:28:33,562 --> 00:28:52,337
The National Domestic Violence Hotline (1–800– 799-SAFE [7233]) is a nonprofit telephone referral service that provides individualized information regarding city-specific shelter locations, counseling resources, and legal advocacy.

228
00:28:52,337 --> 00:28:55,174
# Clinical Evaluation.

229
00:28:55,174 --> 00:29:01,937
Thorough, general physical and pelvic examinations should be completed at the initial prenatal encounter.

230
00:29:01,937 --> 00:29:08,512
The cervix is visualized using a speculum lubricated with warm water or water-based lubricant gel.

231
00:29:08,512 --> 00:29:15,399
Bluish-red passive hyperemia of the cervix is characteristic, but not diagnostic, of pregnancy.

232
00:29:15,399 --> 00:29:23,112
Dilated, occluded cervical glands bulging beneath the ectocervical mucosa— nabothian cysts—may be prominent.

233
00:29:23,112 --> 00:29:27,737
The cervix is not normally dilated except at the external os.

234
00:29:27,737 --> 00:29:37,937
To identify cytological abnormalities, a Pap test is performed according to current guidelines noted in Chapter 66 (p. 1164).

235
00:29:37,937 --> 00:29:46,562
Specimens for identification of Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae are obtained when indicated (p. 181).

236
00:29:46,562 --> 00:29:50,399
Bimanual examination is completed by palpation.

237
00:29:50,399 --> 00:30:02,837
Special attention is given to the consistency, length, and dilation of the cervix; to uterine and adnexal size; to the bony pelvic architecture; and to any vaginal or perineal anomalies.

238
00:30:02,837 --> 00:30:08,074
Often, later in pregnancy, fetal presentation also can be determined.

239
00:30:08,074 --> 00:30:16,274
Lesions of the cervix, vagina, or vulva are further evaluated as needed by colposcopy, biopsy, or culture.

240
00:30:16,274 --> 00:30:25,349
The perianal region is inspected, and digital rectal examination is performed as required for complaints of rectal pain, bleeding, or mass.

241
00:30:25,349 --> 00:30:37,124
Gestational Age Assessment Precise knowledge of gestational age is essential for prenatal care, because several pregnancy complications may develop and optimal treatment will depend on fetal age.

242
00:30:37,124 --> 00:30:41,887
Menstrual history is best confirmed by first-trimester sonography .

243
00:30:41,887 --> 00:30:53,337
That said, gestational age can also be estimated with considerable precision by a carefully performed clinical uterine size examination that is coupled with last menstrual period dating.

244
00:30:53,337 --> 00:31:04,274
Uterine size similar to a small orange roughly correlates with a 6-week gestation; a large orange, with an 8-week pregnancy; and a grapefruit, with one at 12 weeks .

245
00:31:04,274 --> 00:31:06,949
# Laboratory Tests.

246
00:31:06,949 --> 00:31:12,962
Recommended routine tests at the first prenatal encounter are listed in Table 10-1.

247
00:31:12,962 --> 00:31:21,012
Initial blood tests include a complete blood count, a determination of blood type and Rh status, and an antibody screen.

248
00:31:21,012 --> 00:31:29,887
The Institute of Medicine recommends universal human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) testing as a routine part of prenatal care.

249
00:31:29,887 --> 00:31:33,937
This testing is explained to the patient, who may decline.

250
00:31:33,937 --> 00:31:41,462
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018j) continues to support this practice.

251
00:31:41,462 --> 00:31:45,799
If a woman declines, this is recorded in the prenatal record.

252
00:31:45,799 --> 00:31:54,162
All pregnant women are screened also for hepatitis B virus infection, syphilis, and immunity to rubella at the initial visit.

253
00:31:54,162 --> 00:32:07,687
Based on their prospective investigation of 1000 women, Murray and coworkers (2002) concluded that in the absence of hypertension, routine urinalysis beyond the first prenatal visit was unnecessary.

254
00:32:07,687 --> 00:32:18,587
A urine culture is recommended by most, because treating bacteriuria significantly reduces the likelihood of developing symptomatic urinary tract infections in pregnancy .

255
00:32:18,587 --> 00:32:25,662
Cervical Infections C trachomatis is isolated from the cervix in 2 to 13 percent of pregnant women.

256
00:32:25,662 --> 00:32:41,762
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that all women be screened for chlamydia during the first prenatal visit, with additional thirdtrimester testing for those at increased risk.

257
00:32:41,762 --> 00:32:58,137
Risk factors include unmarried status, recent change in sexual partner or multiple concurrent partners, age younger than 25 years, inner-city residence, history or presence of other sexually transmitted diseases, and little or no prenatal care.

258
00:32:58,137 --> 00:33:05,574
For those testing positive, treatment described in Chapter 68 (p. 1212) is followed by a

259
00:33:05,740 --> 00:33:11,402
second testing—a test of cure—3 to 4 weeks after treatment completion.

260
00:33:11,352 --> 00:33:16,577
N gonorrhoeae typically causes cervicitis or urethritis in pregnancy.

261
00:33:16,577 --> 00:33:20,577
Infrequently, it may also cause septic arthritis .

262
00:33:20,577 --> 00:33:25,577
Risk factors for gonorrhea are similar to those for chlamydial infection.

263
00:33:25,577 --> 00:33:43,515
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend that pregnant women with risk factors or those living in an area of high N gonorrhoeae prevalence be screened at the initial prenatal visit and again in the third trimester.

264
00:33:43,515 --> 00:33:49,890
Treatment is given for gonorrhea and simultaneously for possible coexisting chlamydial infection .

265
00:33:49,890 --> 00:33:53,390
Test of cure is recommended following treatment.

266
00:33:53,390 --> 00:33:56,390
# Pregnancy Risk Assessment.

267
00:33:56,390 --> 00:34:00,990
Many factors can adversely affect maternal and fetal well-being.

268
00:34:00,990 --> 00:34:06,640
Some are evident at conception, but many become apparent during the course of pregnancy.

269
00:34:06,640 --> 00:34:15,815
The designation of “high-risk pregnancy” is overly vague for an individual woman and is best avoided if a more specific diagnosis can be assigned.

270
00:34:15,815 --> 00:34:29,352
Some common risk factors for which consultation is recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) are shown in Table 10-3.

271
00:34:29,352 --> 00:34:40,702
Some conditions may require the involvement of a maternal-fetal medicine specialist, geneticist, neonatologist, anesthesiologist, cardiologist, or other specialist.

272
00:34:40,702 --> 00:34:43,002
Table 10-3.

273
00:34:43,002 --> 00:34:49,640
Conditions for Which Maternal-Fetal Medicine Consultation May Be Beneficial Subsequent Prenatal Visits.

274
00:34:49,640 --> 00:34:58,652
These are traditionally scheduled at 4-week intervals until 28 weeks, then every 2 weeks until 36 weeks, and weekly thereafter.

275
00:34:58,652 --> 00:35:07,827
Women with complicated pregnancies—for example, with twins or diabetes—often require return visits at 1- to 2-week intervals .

276
00:35:07,827 --> 00:35:16,002
In 1986, the Department of Health and Human Services convened an expert panel to review the content of prenatal care.

277
00:35:16,002 --> 00:35:21,215
This report was subsequently reevaluated and revised in 2005 .

278
00:35:21,215 --> 00:35:27,890
The panel recommended, among other things, early and continuing risk assessment that is patient specific.

279
00:35:27,890 --> 00:35:45,577
It also endorsed flexibility in clinical visit spacing; health promotion and education, including preconceptional care; medical and psychosocial interventions; standardized documentation; and expanded prenatal care objectives that include family health up to 1 year after birth.

280
00:35:45,577 --> 00:35:57,277
The World Health Organization conducted a multicenter randomized trial with almost 25,000 women that compared routine prenatal care with an experimental model designed to minimize visits .

281
00:35:57,277 --> 00:36:03,152
In the new model, women were seen once in the first trimester and screened for certain risks.

282
00:36:03,152 --> 00:36:11,890
Those without anticipated complications—80 percent of those screened—were seen again at 26, 32, and 38 weeks.

283
00:36:11,890 --> 00:36:19,977
Compared with routine prenatal care, which required a median of eight visits, the new model required a median of only five.

284
00:36:19,977 --> 00:36:28,002
No disadvantages were attributed to the regimen with fewer visits, and these findings are consistent with other randomized trials.

285
00:36:28,002 --> 00:36:30,802
# Prenatal Surveillance.

286
00:36:30,802 --> 00:36:37,115
At each return visit, the well-being of mother and fetus are assessed (see Table 10-1.

287
00:36:37,115 --> 00:36:41,402
Fetal heart rate, growth, and activity are evaluated.

288
00:36:41,402 --> 00:36:45,902
Maternal blood pressure and weight and their extent of change are assessed.

289
00:36:45,902 --> 00:36:55,202
Symptoms such as abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting, bleeding, vaginal fluid leakage, headache, altered vision, and dysuria are sought.

290
00:36:55,202 --> 00:37:03,140
After 20 weeks’ gestation, uterine examination measures size from the symphysis to the fundus with a traditional tape measure.

291
00:37:03,140 --> 00:37:08,827
In late pregnancy, vaginal examination often provides valuable information.

292
00:37:08,827 --> 00:37:24,827
This may include confirmation of the presenting part and its station, clinical estimation of pelvic capacity and configuration, fetal ballottement as a reflection of sufficient amnionic fluid volume, and cervical consistency, effacement, and dilation .

293
00:37:24,827 --> 00:37:34,827
Fundal Height Between 20 and 34 weeks’ gestation, the height of the uterine fundus measured in centimeters correlates closely with gestational age in weeks.

294
00:37:34,827 --> 00:37:40,077
This measurement is used to monitor fetal growth and amnionic fluid volume.

295
00:37:40,077 --> 00:37:42,240
It is measured along the

296
00:37:42,388 --> 00:37:47,350
abdominal wall from the top of the symphysis pubis to the top of the fundus.

297
00:37:47,300 --> 00:37:51,763
Importantly, the bladder must be emptied before fundal measurement.

298
00:37:51,763 --> 00:37:58,913
Obesity or the presence of uterine masses such as leiomyomas also may limit fundal height measurement accuracy.

299
00:37:58,913 --> 00:38:13,175
Moreover, using fundal height alone, fetal-growth restriction may be undiagnosed in up to a third of cases (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021b; Haragan, 2015).

300
00:38:13,175 --> 00:38:27,625
Fetal Heart Sounds Instruments incorporating Doppler ultrasound are usually used to easily detect fetal heart action, and in the absence of maternal obesity, heart sounds are almost always detectable by 10 weeks with such instruments .

301
00:38:27,625 --> 00:38:35,038
The fetal heart rate ranges from 110 to 160 beats per minute and is typically heard as a double sound.

302
00:38:35,038 --> 00:38:46,063
Using a standard nonamplified stethoscope, the fetal heart is audible by 20 weeks in 80 percent of women, and by 22 weeks, heart sounds are expected to be heard in all .

303
00:38:46,063 --> 00:38:54,288
Because the fetus moves freely in amnionic fluid, the site on the maternal abdomen where fetal heart sounds can be heard best will vary.

304
00:38:54,288 --> 00:39:03,475
Additionally, with ultrasonic auscultation, one may hear the funic souffle, which is a sharp, whistling sound that is synchronous with the fetal pulse.

305
00:39:03,475 --> 00:39:09,413
It is caused by the rush of blood through the umbilical arteries and may not be heard consistently.

306
00:39:09,413 --> 00:39:16,025
In contrast, the uterine souffle is a soft, blowing sound that is synchronous with the maternal pulse.

307
00:39:16,025 --> 00:39:23,850
It is produced by the passage of blood through the dilated uterine vessels and is heard most distinctly near the lower portion of the uterus.

308
00:39:23,850 --> 00:39:31,600
Sonography Ultrasound imaging provides invaluable information regarding fetal anatomy, growth, and well-being.

309
00:39:31,600 --> 00:39:38,763
As such, it is recommended that all pregnant women be offered at least one prenatal sonographic examination .

310
00:39:38,763 --> 00:39:45,100
Continuing trends suggest that the number of these examinations performed per pregnancy is increasing.

311
00:39:45,100 --> 00:39:55,638
Data from commercial insurance plans indicate that even low-risk pregnancies receive an average of 4 to 5 ultrasound examinations (O’Keeffe, 2013).

312
00:39:55,638 --> 00:40:00,350
Sonography should be performed only for valid medical indications.

313
00:40:00,350 --> 00:40:10,638
Additionally, needed information is obtained using the lowest possible ultrasound exposure settings, which is the as low as reasonably achievable (ALARA) principle .

314
00:40:10,638 --> 00:40:13,900
# Subsequent Laboratory Tests.

315
00:40:13,900 --> 00:40:18,325
If initial results were normal, most tests need not be repeated.

316
00:40:18,325 --> 00:40:31,650
Hematocrit or hemoglobin determination, along with serology for syphilis if it is prevalent in the population, is repeated at 28 to 32 weeks (Hollier, 2003; Kiss, 2004).

317
00:40:31,650 --> 00:40:41,988
For women at increased risk for HIV acquisition during pregnancy, repeat testing is recommended in the third trimester, preferably before 36 weeks .

318
00:40:41,988 --> 00:40:50,913
Similarly, women who engage in behaviors that place them at high risk for hepatitis B virus infection are retested at the time of delivery.

319
00:40:50,913 --> 00:40:59,750
Women who are D (Rh) negative and are unsensitized should have an antibody screening test repeated at 28 to 29 weeks.

320
00:40:59,750 --> 00:41:04,450
Anti-D immunoglobulin is given if they remain unsensitized .

321
00:41:04,450 --> 00:41:25,150
Group B Streptococcal Infection The CDC (2010b) recommends that vaginal and rectal group B streptococcal (GBS) cultures be obtained in all women between 35 and 37 weeks’ gestation, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020f) endorses this recommendation.

322
00:41:25,150 --> 00:41:31,538
Intrapartum antimicrobial prophylaxis is provided to those whose culture results are positive.

323
00:41:31,538 --> 00:41:41,188
Women with GBS bacteriuria, preterm labor, or a previous infant with invasive disease are given empirical intrapartum prophylaxis.

324
00:41:41,188 --> 00:41:45,450
Trials are in progress to test an investigational vaccine .

325
00:41:45,450 --> 00:41:51,488
These infections are described further in Chapter 67 (p. 1194).

326
00:41:51,488 --> 00:42:01,975
Gestational Diabetes All pregnant women are screened for gestational diabetes mellitus, whether by history, clinical factors, or routine laboratory testing.

327
00:42:01,975 --> 00:42:12,625
Although laboratory testing between 24 and 28 weeks’ gestation is the most sensitive approach, there may be women at low risk who are less likely to benefit from testing .

328
00:42:12,625 --> 00:42:18,313
Gestational diabetes is discussed in Chapter 60 (p. 1079).

329
00:42:18,313 --> 00:42:21,725
Genetic Screening Serum screening for fetal

330
00:42:21,892 --> 00:42:35,279
aneuploidy is routinely offered to all pregnant women—in the first trimester at 10 to 14 weeks, in the second trimester at 15 to 20 weeks, or as cell-free DNA screening at any point after 10 weeks .

331
00:42:35,229 --> 00:42:51,192
Additionally, the College recommends that both cystic fibrosis carrier screening and screening for spinal muscular atrophy should be offered to all women considering pregnancy or who are currently pregnant, provided that carrier or disease status is not already known .

332
00:42:51,192 --> 00:43:00,754
Historically, carrier screening for selected genetic abnormalities was offered only to women at increased risk based on ethnic or racial background.

333
00:43:00,754 --> 00:43:06,317
One example is screening for Tay-Sachs disease in those of Ashkenazi Jewish descent.

334
00:43:06,317 --> 00:43:14,842
However, given our increasingly diverse, multiethnic society, previous assumptions about carrier risk may no longer apply.

335
00:43:14,842 --> 00:43:24,329
Although ethnicity-specific carrier screening remains an option, providers should also consider panethnic and expanded carrier screening strategies .

336
00:43:24,329 --> 00:43:29,204
These are discussed further in Chapter 17 (p. 342).

337
00:43:29,204 --> 00:43:37,017
All genetic screening is optional, and ideally, genetic carrier screening and counseling should be performed before pregnancy.

338
00:43:37,017 --> 00:43:45,617
Neural-Tube Defects Traditionally, screening for neural-tube defects has been performed as part of second-trimester aneuploidy screening.

339
00:43:45,617 --> 00:43:55,254
An elevation of maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) levels then prompted additional evaluation with ultrasound and/or amniocentesis.

340
00:43:55,254 --> 00:44:03,029
With the advent of other screening modalities for aneuploidy, second-trimester MSAFP testing is less frequently obtained.

341
00:44:03,029 --> 00:44:11,879
For example, the expansion of second-trimester fetal anatomical surveillance has been used to screen and identify neural-tube defects .

342
00:44:11,879 --> 00:44:14,117
Nutritional Counseling.

343
00:44:14,117 --> 00:44:17,092
# Weight Gain Recommendations.

344
00:44:17,092 --> 00:44:31,554
In 2009, the Institute of Medicine and National Research Council revised guidelines for weight gain in pregnancy and continued to stratify suggested weight gain ranges based on prepregnancy body mass index (BMI) .

345
00:44:31,554 --> 00:44:37,192
The same recommendations apply to women in all age, race, and ethnic groups.

346
00:44:37,192 --> 00:44:44,229
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018n) endorses these measures.

347
00:44:44,229 --> 00:44:46,542
Table 10-4.

348
00:44:46,542 --> 00:44:56,879
Recommendations for Total and Rate of Weight Gain During Pregnancy When the initial Institute of Medicine guidelines were formulated, concern focused on low-birthweight newborns.

349
00:44:56,879 --> 00:45:01,642
However, current emphasis is directed to the obesity epidemic.

350
00:45:01,642 --> 00:45:10,879
The specific and relatively narrow range of recommended weight gains for obese women emphasizes the renewed interest in lower weight gains during pregnancy.

351
00:45:10,879 --> 00:45:23,479
Obesity is associated with significantly greater risks for gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, gestational diabetes, macrosomia, cesarean delivery, and other complications .

352
00:45:23,479 --> 00:45:27,267
The risk appears proportionate to prenatal weight gain.

353
00:45:27,267 --> 00:45:38,079
In a Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network cohort of more than 29,000 pregnant women, 51 percent had weight gain above and 21 percent below the guidelines .

354
00:45:38,079 --> 00:45:43,592
Those with excessive weight gain had higher rates of hypertension and cesarean delivery.

355
00:45:43,592 --> 00:45:47,792
This risk is even greater in chronically hypertensive women .

356
00:45:47,792 --> 00:46:09,092
Conversely, among 100,000 women with normal prepregnancy BMI, DeVader and colleagues (2007) found that those who gained <25 lb during pregnancy had a lower risk for preeclampsia, failed induction, cephalopelvic disproportion, cesarean delivery, and large-for-gestational age neonates.

357
00:46:09,092 --> 00:46:15,179
This cohort, however, had an increased risk for small-for-gestational age newborns.

358
00:46:15,179 --> 00:46:18,004
# Severe Undernutrition.

359
00:46:18,004 --> 00:46:26,904
Meaningful studies of nutrition in human pregnancy are exceedingly difficult to design because experimental dietary deficiency is not ethical.

360
00:46:26,904 --> 00:46:42,304
In those instances in which severe nutritional deficiencies have been induced as a consequence of social, economic, or political disaster, coincidental events have often created many variables, the effects of which are not amenable to quantification.

361
00:46:42,304 --> 00:46:53,004
Some past experiences suggest, however, that in otherwise healthy women, a state of near starvation is required to establish clear differences in pregnancy outcome.

362
00:46:53,004 --> 00:46:57,592
These are discussed in Chapter 47 (p. 827).

363
00:46:57,592 --> 00:46:59,529
# Weight

364
00:46:59,692 --> 00:47:02,254
Retention After Pregnancy.

365
00:47:02,204 --> 00:47:07,729
Not all the weight gained during pregnancy is lost during and immediately after delivery.

366
00:47:07,729 --> 00:47:15,729
Schauberger and coworkers (1992) studied prenatal and postpartum weights in 795 women.

367
00:47:15,729 --> 00:47:21,479
Their average weight gain was 28.6 lb or 12.9 kg.

368
00:47:21,479 --> 00:47:34,842
As shown in Figure 10-3, most maternal weight loss was at delivery —approximately 12 lb or 5.4 kg—and in the ensuing 2 weeks— approximately 9 lb or 4 kg.

369
00:47:34,842 --> 00:47:42,567
An additional 5.5 lb or 2.5 kg was lost between 2 weeks and 6 months postpartum.

370
00:47:42,567 --> 00:47:48,154
Thus, average retained pregnancy weight was 2.1 lb or 1 kg.

371
00:47:48,154 --> 00:47:58,817
Excessive weight gain is manifest by accrual of fat and may be partially retained as long-term fat (Berggren, 2016; Widen, 2015).

372
00:47:58,817 --> 00:48:04,579
Overall, the more weight that was gained during pregnancy, the more that was lost postpartum.

373
00:48:04,579 --> 00:48:09,004
And breastfeeding duration is inversely related to weight retention .

374
00:48:09,004 --> 00:48:11,329
Figure 10-3.

375
00:48:11,329 --> 00:48:16,629
Cumulative weight loss from last antepartum visit to 6 months postpartum.

376
00:48:16,629 --> 00:48:21,142
On average, 1 kg will be retained after pregnancy.

377
00:48:21,142 --> 00:48:27,442
Significantly different from 2-week weight loss; Significantly different from 6-week weight loss.

378
00:48:27,442 --> 00:48:36,104
Redrawn with permission from Schauberger CW, Rooney BL, Brimer LM: Factors that influence weight loss in the puerperium.

379
00:48:36,104 --> 00:48:41,417
Obstet Gynecol 79:424, 1992.

380
00:48:41,417 --> 00:48:46,042
# Dietary Reference Intakes—Recommended Allowances.

381
00:48:46,042 --> 00:48:57,379
Periodically, the Institute of Medicine (2006, 2011) publishes recommended dietary allowances, including those for pregnant or lactating women.

382
00:48:57,379 --> 00:49:02,592
Some of its latest recommendations are summarized in Table 10-5.

383
00:49:02,592 --> 00:49:09,654
Certain prenatal vitamin–mineral supplements may lead to intakes well in excess of the recommended allowances.

384
00:49:09,654 --> 00:49:18,892
Moreover, the use of excessive supplements, which often are self-prescribed, has led to concern regarding nutrient toxicities during pregnancy.

385
00:49:18,892 --> 00:49:28,704
Those with potentially toxic effects include iron, zinc, selenium, and vitamins A, B6, C, and D. Table 10-5.

386
00:49:28,704 --> 00:49:34,867
Recommended Daily Dietary Allowances for Pregnant and Lactating Women # Calories.

387
00:49:34,867 --> 00:49:43,692
As shown in Figure 10-4, pregnancy requires an additional 80,000 kcal, mostly during the last 20 weeks.

388
00:49:43,692 --> 00:49:51,529
To meet this demand, a caloric increase of 100 to 300 kcal/d is recommended during pregnancy .

389
00:49:51,529 --> 00:49:57,467
This greater intake, however, should not be divided equally during the course of pregnancy.

390
00:49:57,467 --> 00:50:13,079
The Institute of Medicine (2006) recommends adding 0, 340, and 452 kcal/d to the estimated nonpregnant energy requirements in the first, second, and third trimesters, respectively.

391
00:50:13,079 --> 00:50:18,629
The addition of 1000 kcal/d or more results in fat accrual .

392
00:50:18,629 --> 00:50:20,954
Figure 10-4.

393
00:50:20,954 --> 00:50:24,754
Cumulative kilocalories required for pregnancy.

394
00:50:24,754 --> 00:50:36,467
Redrawn with permission from Chamberlain G, Broughton-Pipkin F [eds]: Clinical Physiology in Obstetrics, 3rd ed. Oxford, Blackwell Science, 1998.

395
00:50:36,467 --> 00:50:45,042
Whenever caloric intake is inadequate, protein is metabolized rather than being spared for its vital role in fetal growth and development.

396
00:50:45,042 --> 00:50:54,567
Total physiological requirements during pregnancy are not necessarily the sum of ordinary nonpregnant requirements plus those specific to pregnancy.

397
00:50:54,567 --> 00:51:02,892
For example, the additional energy required during pregnancy may be compensated in whole or in part by reduced physical activity .

398
00:51:02,892 --> 00:51:05,017
# Protein.

399
00:51:05,017 --> 00:51:15,329
Protein requirements rise to meet the demands for growth and remodeling of the fetus, placenta, uterus, and breasts, and for the expanded maternal blood volume .

400
00:51:15,329 --> 00:51:23,954
During the second half of pregnancy, approximately 1000 g of protein are deposited, amounting to 5 to 6 g/d .

401
00:51:23,954 --> 00:51:31,004
To accomplish this, protein intake that approximates 1 g/kg/d is recommended .

402
00:51:31,004 --> 00:51:34,754
Data suggest this should be doubled in late gestation .

403
00:51:34,754 --> 00:51:42,792
Most amino-acid levels in maternal plasma fall markedly, including ornithine, glycine, taurine, and proline .

404
00:51:42,792 --> 00:51:49,192
Exceptions during pregnancy are glutamic acid and alanine, the concentrations of which rise.

405
00:51:49,192 --> 00:51:57,304
Preferably, most protein is supplied from animal sources, such as meat, milk, eggs, cheese, poultry, and fish.

406
00:51:57,304 --> 00:52:01,317
These furnish amino acids in optimal combinations.

407
00:52:01,317 --> 00:52:10,529
Milk and dairy products are considered nearly ideal sources of nutrients, especially protein and calcium, for pregnant or lactating women.

408
00:52:10,529 --> 00:52:15,317
Ingestion of specific fish and potential methylmercury toxicity

409
00:52:15,484 --> 00:52:18,821
are discussed later (p. 188).

410
00:52:18,771 --> 00:52:20,921
# Minerals.

411
00:52:20,921 --> 00:52:29,359
The intakes recommended by the Institute of Medicine (2006) for various minerals are listed in Table 10-5.

412
00:52:29,359 --> 00:52:39,571
With the exception of iron and iodine, practically all diets that supply sufficient calories for appropriate weight gain will contain enough minerals to prevent deficiency.

413
00:52:39,571 --> 00:52:47,596
Iron requirements substantively rise during pregnancy, and reasons for this are discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 60).

414
00:52:47,596 --> 00:53:00,934
Of the approximately 300 mg of iron transferred to the fetus and placenta and the 500 mg incorporated into the expanding maternal hemoglobin mass, nearly all is used after midpregnancy.

415
00:53:00,934 --> 00:53:09,346
During that time, iron requirements imposed by pregnancy and maternal excretion total approximately 7 mg/d .

416
00:53:09,346 --> 00:53:14,871
Few women have sufficient iron stores or dietary intake to supply this amount.

417
00:53:14,871 --> 00:53:31,996
Thus, the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) endorse the recommendation by the National Academy of Sciences that at least 27 mg of elemental iron be supplemented daily to pregnant women.

418
00:53:31,996 --> 00:53:35,684
This amount is contained in most prenatal vitamins.

419
00:53:35,684 --> 00:53:51,721
As little as 30 mg of elemental iron, supplied as ferrous gluconate, sulfate, or fumarate, and taken daily throughout the latter half of pregnancy, provides sufficient iron to meet pregnancy requirements and protect preexisting iron stores .

420
00:53:51,721 --> 00:53:56,284
This amount will also provide for iron requirements of lactation.

421
00:53:56,284 --> 00:54:05,971
Notably, in iron preparations, the number of milligrams of compound is followed by the milligrams of elemental iron, which is enclosed by parentheses.

422
00:54:05,971 --> 00:54:21,459
The pregnant woman may benefit from 60 to 100 mg of elemental iron per day if she is obese, has a multifetal gestation, begins supplementation late in pregnancy, takes iron irregularly, or has a depressed hemoglobin level.

423
00:54:21,459 --> 00:54:28,384
The woman who is overtly anemic from iron deficiency responds well to oral supplementation with iron salts.

424
00:54:28,384 --> 00:54:34,134
In response, serum ferritin levels rise more than the hemoglobin concentration .

425
00:54:34,134 --> 00:54:40,834
Iodine also is needed, and the recommended iodine allowance is 220 µg/d .

426
00:54:40,834 --> 00:54:50,246
The use of iodized salt and bread products is recommended during pregnancy to offset the increased fetal requirements and maternal renal losses of iodine.

427
00:54:50,246 --> 00:55:02,784
Despite this, iodine intake has declined substantially in the past 15 years, and in some areas it is probably inadequate (Caldwell, 2013; Chittimoju, 2019).

428
00:55:02,784 --> 00:55:12,321
Severe maternal iodine deficiency predisposes offspring to endemic cretinism, which is characterized by multiple severe neurological defects.

429
00:55:12,321 --> 00:55:21,509
In parts of China and Africa, where this condition is common, iodide supplementation very early in pregnancy prevents some cretinism cases .

430
00:55:21,509 --> 00:55:27,609
To obviate this, most prenatal supplements now contain various quantities of iodine .

431
00:55:27,609 --> 00:55:33,309
Calcium is retained by the pregnant woman during gestation and approximates 30 g.

432
00:55:33,309 --> 00:55:37,296
Most of this is deposited in the fetus late in pregnancy .

433
00:55:37,296 --> 00:55:48,159
This amount of calcium represents only approximately 2.5 percent of total maternal calcium, most of which is in bone and can readily be mobilized for fetal growth.

434
00:55:48,159 --> 00:55:55,021
As another potential use, routine calcium supplementation to prevent preeclampsia is ineffective .

435
00:55:55,021 --> 00:56:01,759
Zinc deficiency if severe may lead to poor appetite, suboptimal growth, and impaired wound healing.

436
00:56:01,759 --> 00:56:07,409
During pregnancy, the recommended daily intake approximates 12 mg.

437
00:56:07,409 --> 00:56:13,384
But, the safe level of zinc supplementation for pregnant women is not clearly established.

438
00:56:13,384 --> 00:56:16,659
Vegetarians have lower zinc intakes .

439
00:56:16,659 --> 00:56:27,034
The bulk of studies support zinc supplementation only in zinc-deficient women in poor-resource countries (Nossier, 2015; Ota, 2015).

440
00:56:27,034 --> 00:56:32,321
Magnesium deficiency as a consequence of pregnancy has not been recognized.

441
00:56:32,321 --> 00:56:41,884
Undoubtedly, during prolonged illness with no magnesium intake, the plasma level might become critically low, as it would in the absence of pregnancy.

442
00:56:41,884 --> 00:56:48,134
We have observed this deficiency during pregnancies in some with previous intestinal bypass surgery.

443
00:56:48,134 --> 00:57:04,721
Instead, as a preventive agent, Sibai and colleagues (1989) randomly assigned 400 normotensive primigravidas to receive 365-mg elemental magnesium supplementation or placebo tablets from 13 to 24

444
00:57:04,876 --> 00:57:06,913
weeks’ gestation.

445
00:57:06,863 --> 00:57:11,363
Supplementation did not improve any measures of pregnancy outcome.

446
00:57:11,363 --> 00:57:19,751
Trace metals include copper, selenium, chromium, and manganese, which all have important roles in certain enzyme functions.

447
00:57:19,751 --> 00:57:23,576
In general, most are provided by an average diet.

448
00:57:23,576 --> 00:57:31,288
Selenium deficiency is manifested by a frequently fatal cardiomyopathy in young children and reproductive-aged women.

449
00:57:31,288 --> 00:57:37,588
Conversely, selenium toxicity resulting from oversupplementation also has been observed.

450
00:57:37,588 --> 00:57:41,788
Selenium supplementation is not needed in American women.

451
00:57:41,788 --> 00:57:50,551
Potassium concentrations in maternal plasma decline by approximately 0.5 mEq/L by midpregnancy .

452
00:57:50,551 --> 00:57:56,626
Potassium deficiency develops in the same circumstances as in nonpregnant individuals.

453
00:57:56,626 --> 00:58:01,463
One common example in pregnant women is hyperemesis gravidarum.

454
00:58:01,463 --> 00:58:06,163
Fluoride metabolism is not altered appreciably during pregnancy .

455
00:58:06,163 --> 00:58:17,638
Horowitz and Heifetz (1967) concluded that no additional offspring benefits accrued from maternal ingestion of fluoridated water if the newborn ingested such water from birth.

456
00:58:17,638 --> 00:58:31,226
Sa Roriz Fonteles and associates (2005) studied microdrill biopsies of deciduous teeth and concluded that antenatal fluoride provided no additional fluoride uptake compared with postnatal fluoride alone.

457
00:58:31,226 --> 00:58:38,913
Finally, supplemental fluoride ingested by lactating women does not raise the fluoride concentration in breast milk .

458
00:58:38,913 --> 00:58:41,126
# Vitamins.

459
00:58:41,126 --> 00:58:47,151
The increased requirements for most vitamins during pregnancy shown in Table 10-5.

460
00:58:47,151 --> 00:58:53,051
usually are supplied by any general diet that provides adequate calories and protein.

461
00:58:53,051 --> 00:59:03,863
The exception is folic acid during times of unusual requirements, such as pregnancy complicated by protracted vomiting, hemolytic anemia, or multiple fetuses.

462
00:59:03,863 --> 00:59:16,601
That said, in impoverished countries, routine multivitamin supplementation reduced the incidence of lowbirthweight and growth-restricted fetuses but did not alter preterm delivery or perinatal mortality rates .

463
00:59:16,601 --> 00:59:22,413
Folic acid supplementation in early pregnancy can lower neural-tube defect risks .

464
00:59:22,413 --> 00:59:38,338
For example, the CDC (2004) estimated that the number of affected pregnancies had decreased from 4000 per year to approximately 3000 per year after mandatory fortification of cereal products with folic acid in 1998.

465
00:59:38,338 --> 00:59:47,538
Perhaps more than half of all neural-tube defects can be prevented with daily intake of 400 µg of folic acid throughout the periconceptional period .

466
00:59:47,538 --> 00:59:53,601
Evidence also suggests that folate insufficiency has a global effect on brain development .

467
00:59:53,601 --> 01:00:05,551
Putting 140 µg of folic acid into each 100 g of grain products may increase the folic acid intake of the average American woman of childbearing age by 100 µg/d.

468
01:00:05,551 --> 01:00:13,051
Because nutritional sources alone are insufficient, however, folic acid supplementation is still recommended.

469
01:00:13,051 --> 01:00:27,238
A woman with a prior child with a neural-tube defect can reduce the 2to 5-percent recurrence risk by more than 70 percent with a daily 4-mg folic acid supplement taken during the month before conception and during the first trimester.

470
01:00:27,238 --> 01:00:41,101
As emphasized by the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017), this dose should be consumed as a separate supplement and not as multivitamin tablets.

471
01:00:41,101 --> 01:00:45,701
This practice avoids excessive intake of fat-soluble vitamins.

472
01:00:45,701 --> 01:00:56,451
Vitamin A, although essential, has been associated with congenital malformations when taken during pregnancy in high doses (>10,000 IU/d).

473
01:00:56,451 --> 01:01:05,001
These malformations are similar to those produced by the vitamin A derivative isotretinoin (Accutane), which is a potent teratogen .

474
01:01:05,001 --> 01:01:12,363
Beta-carotene, the precursor of vitamin A found in fruits and vegetables, does not produce vitamin A toxicity.

475
01:01:12,363 --> 01:01:18,976
Most prenatal vitamins contain vitamin A in doses considerably below the teratogenic threshold.

476
01:01:18,976 --> 01:01:27,601
Dietary intake of vitamin A in the United States appears to be adequate, and additional supplementation is not routinely recommended.

477
01:01:27,601 --> 01:01:33,988
In contrast, vitamin A deficiency is an endemic nutritional problem in the developing world .

478
01:01:33,988 --> 01:01:44,688
Vitamin A deficiency, whether overt or subclinical, is associated with night blindness and with an increased risk of maternal anemia and spontaneous preterm birth .

479
01:01:44,688 --> 01:01:46,238
Vitamin

480
01:01:46,396 --> 01:01:55,646
B12 plasma levels drop in normal pregnancy, mostly as a result of reduced plasma levels of their carrier proteins—transcobalamins.

481
01:01:55,596 --> 01:02:04,933
Vitamin B12 occurs naturally only in foods of animal origin, and strict vegetarians may give birth to neonates whose B12 stores are low.

482
01:02:04,933 --> 01:02:14,383
Likewise, because breast milk of a vegetarian mother contains little vitamin B12, the deficiency may become profound in the breastfed infant .

483
01:02:14,383 --> 01:02:20,771
Excessive ingestion of vitamin C also can lead to a functional deficiency of vitamin B12.

484
01:02:20,771 --> 01:02:29,246
Although its role is still controversial, vitamin B12 deficiency may be an independent factor associated with neural-tube defects .

485
01:02:29,246 --> 01:02:35,658
Vitamin B6, which is pyridoxine, does not require supplementation in most gravidas .

486
01:02:35,658 --> 01:02:42,183
For women at high risk for inadequate nutrition, a daily 2-mg supplement is recommended.

487
01:02:42,183 --> 01:02:53,283
As discussed on page 191, vitamin B6, when combined with the antihistamine doxylamine, is helpful in many cases of nausea and vomiting of pregnancy.

488
01:02:53,283 --> 01:03:02,158
Vitamin C allowances during pregnancy are 80 to 85 mg/d— approximately 20 percent more than when nonpregnant .

489
01:03:02,158 --> 01:03:08,233
A reasonable diet should readily provide this amount, and supplementation is unnecessary .

490
01:03:08,233 --> 01:03:13,983
Maternal plasma levels decline during pregnancy, whereas cord blood levels are higher.

491
01:03:13,983 --> 01:03:18,458
This is a phenomenon observed with most water-soluble vitamins.

492
01:03:18,458 --> 01:03:21,671
Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin.

493
01:03:21,671 --> 01:03:30,983
After being metabolized to its active form, it boosts the efficiency of intestinal calcium absorption and promotes bone mineralization and growth.

494
01:03:30,983 --> 01:03:40,471
Unlike most vitamins that are obtained exclusively from dietary intake, vitamin D is also synthesized endogenously with exposure to sunlight.

495
01:03:40,471 --> 01:03:44,221
Vitamin D deficiency is common during pregnancy.

496
01:03:44,221 --> 01:03:54,421
This is especially true in high-risk groups such as women with limited sun exposure, vegetarians, and ethnic minorities—particularly those with darker skin .

497
01:03:54,421 --> 01:04:02,021
Maternal deficiency can cause disordered skeletal homeostasis, congenital rickets, and fractures in the newborn .

498
01:04:02,021 --> 01:04:09,646
However, vitamin D supplementation in women with asthma may decrease the likelihood of childhood asthma in their offspring .

499
01:04:09,646 --> 01:04:22,846
The Food and Nutrition Board of the Institute of Medicine (2011) established that an adequate intake of vitamin D during pregnancy and lactation was 15 µg/d (600 IU/d).

500
01:04:22,846 --> 01:04:30,546
In women suspected of having vitamin D deficiency, serum levels of 25hydroxyvitamin D can be obtained.

501
01:04:30,546 --> 01:04:35,483
Even then, the optimal levels in pregnancy have not been established .

502
01:04:35,483 --> 01:04:38,971
# Pragmatic Nutritional Surveillance.

503
01:04:38,971 --> 01:04:53,583
Although researchers continue to study the ideal nutritional regimen for the pregnant woman and her fetus, basic tenets for the clinician include: 1. Advise the pregnant woman to eat food types she wants in reasonable amounts and salted to taste.

504
01:04:53,583 --> 01:04:59,196
2. Ensure that food is amply available for socioeconomically deprived women.

505
01:04:59,196 --> 01:05:04,996
3. Monitor weight gain and align goals with the Institute of Medicine recommendations.

506
01:05:04,996 --> 01:05:12,658
4. Explore food intake by dietary recall periodically to discover the occasional nutritionally errant diet.

507
01:05:12,658 --> 01:05:19,646
5. Give tablets of simple iron salts that provide at least 30 mg of elemental iron daily.

508
01:05:19,646 --> 01:05:24,358
Give folate supplementation before and in the early weeks of pregnancy.

509
01:05:24,358 --> 01:05:29,796
Provide iodine supplementation in areas of known dietary insufficiency.

510
01:05:29,796 --> 01:05:38,633
6. Recheck the hematocrit or hemoglobin concentration at 28 to 32 weeks’ gestation to detect significant anemia.

511
01:05:38,633 --> 01:05:40,696
Common Concerns.

512
01:05:40,696 --> 01:05:42,871
# Employment.

513
01:05:42,871 --> 01:05:47,708
More than half of the children in the United States are born to working mothers.

514
01:05:47,708 --> 01:05:52,096
Several federal laws have been passed to protect pregnant workers.

515
01:05:52,096 --> 01:05:59,233
These prohibit employers from excluding women from job categories on the basis that they are or might become pregnant.

516
01:05:59,233 --> 01:06:10,421
The Family and Medical Leave Act of 1993 requires that covered employers must grant up to 12 work weeks of unpaid leave to an employee for the birth and care of a newborn child.

517
01:06:10,421 --> 01:06:16,371
In the absence of complications, most women can continue to work until labor onset .

518
01:06:16,371 --> 01:06:21,571
Some types of work, however, may increase pregnancy complication risks.

519
01:06:21,571 --> 01:06:30,746
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b), risks of preterm birth are slightly to modestly

520
01:06:30,916 --> 01:06:40,966
increased with standing or walking at work >3 hours daily, lifting and carrying >5 kg, or physically exerting oneself at work.

521
01:06:40,916 --> 01:06:48,953
In a prospective study of more than 900 healthy nulliparas, women who worked had a fivefold higher risk of preeclampsia .

522
01:06:48,953 --> 01:06:55,216
Thus, any occupation that subjects the gravida to severe physical strain should be avoided.

523
01:06:55,216 --> 01:07:00,791
Ideally, no work or play is continued to the extent that undue fatigue develops.

524
01:07:00,791 --> 01:07:04,128
Adequate periods of rest should be provided.

525
01:07:04,128 --> 01:07:06,441
# Exercise.

526
01:07:06,441 --> 01:07:14,728
In general, pregnant women do not need to limit exercise, provided they do not become excessively fatigued or risk injury .

527
01:07:14,728 --> 01:07:23,091
Clapp and associates (2000) reported that both placental size and birthweight were significantly greater in women who exercised.

528
01:07:23,091 --> 01:07:29,716
Duncombe and coworkers (2006) reported similar findings in 148 women.

529
01:07:29,716 --> 01:07:43,691
In contrast, Magann and colleagues (2002) prospectively analyzed exercise behavior in 750 healthy women and found that working women who exercised had smaller neonates and more dysfunctional labors.

530
01:07:43,691 --> 01:07:53,966
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020a) advises a thorough clinical evaluation before recommending an exercise program.

531
01:07:53,966 --> 01:08:06,378
In the absence of contraindications listed in Table 10-6, pregnant women are encouraged to engage in regular, moderate-intensity physical activity for at least 150 minutes each week.

532
01:08:06,378 --> 01:08:12,053
Such activity has been shown to not adversely alter uterine artery Doppler studies .

533
01:08:12,053 --> 01:08:16,591
Each activity should be reviewed individually for its potential risk.

534
01:08:16,591 --> 01:08:24,041
Examples of safe activities are walking, running, swimming, stationary cycling, and lowimpact aerobics.

535
01:08:24,041 --> 01:08:30,028
However, they should refrain from activities with a high risk of falling or abdominal trauma.

536
01:08:30,028 --> 01:08:36,653
Similarly, scuba diving is avoided because the fetus is at increased risk for decompression sickness .

537
01:08:36,653 --> 01:08:39,028
Table 10-6.

538
01:08:39,028 --> 01:08:49,841
Some Contraindications to Exercise During Pregnancy In the setting of certain pregnancy complications, it is wise to abstain from exercise and even limit physical activity.

539
01:08:49,841 --> 01:09:01,828
Some women with pregnancyassociated hypertensive disorders, preterm labor, placenta previa, or severe cardiac or pulmonary disease may accrue advantages from being sedentary.

540
01:09:01,828 --> 01:09:08,253
Also, those with multiple or suspected growth-restricted fetuses may be served by greater rest.

541
01:09:08,253 --> 01:09:10,966
# Seafood Consumption.

542
01:09:10,966 --> 01:09:18,241
Fish are an excellent source of protein, are low in saturated fats, and contain omega-3 fatty acids.

543
01:09:18,241 --> 01:09:30,741
It is recommended that pregnant women ingest 8 to 12 ounces of fish weekly, but no more than 6 ounces of albacore or “white” tuna (U.S. Environmental Protection Agency, 2019).

544
01:09:30,741 --> 01:09:42,241
Because nearly all fish and shellfish contain trace amounts of mercury, pregnant and lactating women are advised to avoid specific types of fish with potentially high methylmercury levels.

545
01:09:42,241 --> 01:09:47,016
These include shark, swordfish, king mackerel, and tile fish.

546
01:09:47,016 --> 01:09:54,841
If the mercury content of locally caught fish is unknown, overall fish consumption should be limited to 6 ounces per week.

547
01:09:54,841 --> 01:10:00,553
Finally, to help avert listeriosis, eating raw or undercooked fish is avoided .

548
01:10:00,553 --> 01:10:02,878
# Lead Screening.

549
01:10:02,878 --> 01:10:11,041
Maternal lead exposure is associated with several adverse maternal and fetal outcomes across a range of maternal blood lead levels .

550
01:10:11,041 --> 01:10:19,503
These include gestational hypertension, miscarriage, low birthweight, and neurodevelopmental impairments in exposed pregnancies.

551
01:10:19,503 --> 01:10:23,453
The levels at which these risks rise remains unclear.

552
01:10:23,453 --> 01:10:37,291
However, recognizing that such exposure remains a significant health issue for reproductive-aged women, the CDC (2010a) provides guidance for screening and managing exposed pregnant and lactating women.

553
01:10:37,291 --> 01:10:49,166
These guidelines, which have been endorsed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018f), recommend blood lead testing only if a risk factor is identified.

554
01:10:49,166 --> 01:10:54,353
If the levels are >5 µg/dL, the lead source is sought and removed.

555
01:10:54,353 --> 01:10:57,253
Subsequent blood levels are obtained.

556
01:10:57,253 --> 01:11:06,841
Blood lead levels >45 µg/dL are consistent with lead poisoning, and women in this group may be candidates for chelation therapy.

557
01:11:06,841 --> 01:11:12,891
Affected pregnancies are best managed in consultation with lead poisoning treatment experts.

558
01:11:12,891 --> 01:11:15,666
National and state resources are

559
01:11:15,820 --> 01:11:24,182
available at the CDC website: www.cdc.gov/nceh/lead/.

560
01:11:24,132 --> 01:11:27,207
# Automobile and Air Travel.

561
01:11:27,207 --> 01:11:34,932
Pregnant women are encouraged to wear properly positioned three-point restraints as protection against automobile accident injury .

562
01:11:34,932 --> 01:11:40,695
The lap portion of the restraining belt is placed under the abdomen and across her upper thighs.

563
01:11:40,695 --> 01:11:43,395
The belt should be comfortably snug.

564
01:11:43,395 --> 01:11:47,582
The shoulder belt also is firmly positioned between the breasts.

565
01:11:47,582 --> 01:11:51,195
Airbags should not be disabled for the pregnant woman.

566
01:11:51,195 --> 01:11:57,645
In general, air travel in a properly pressurized aircraft has no harmful effect on pregnancy.

567
01:11:57,645 --> 01:12:11,957
Thus, in the absence of obstetrical or medical complications, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a) has concluded that pregnant women can safely fly up to 36 weeks’ gestation.

568
01:12:11,957 --> 01:12:17,907
It is recommended that they observe the same precautions for air travel as the general population.

569
01:12:17,907 --> 01:12:20,532
Seatbelts are used while seated.

570
01:12:20,532 --> 01:12:29,170
Support stockings, periodic lower extremity movement, and at least hourly ambulation help lower the venous thromboembolism threat.

571
01:12:29,170 --> 01:12:39,882
Significant risks with travel, especially international travel, are infectious disease acquisition and development of complications remote from adequate health-care resources.

572
01:12:39,882 --> 01:12:42,020
# Coitus.

573
01:12:42,020 --> 01:12:47,095
In healthy pregnant women, sexual intercourse usually is not harmful.

574
01:12:47,095 --> 01:12:53,832
Whenever miscarriage, placenta previa, or preterm labor threatens, however, coitus is avoided.

575
01:12:53,832 --> 01:13:03,557
Nearly 10,000 women enrolled in a prospective investigation by the Vaginal Infection and Prematurity Study Group were interviewed regarding sexual activity .

576
01:13:03,557 --> 01:13:08,220
They reported a decreased frequency of coitus with advancing gestation.

577
01:13:08,220 --> 01:13:13,670
By 36 weeks, 72 percent had intercourse less than once weekly.

578
01:13:13,670 --> 01:13:18,645
The decline is attributed to lower desire and fear of harming the pregnancy .

579
01:13:18,645 --> 01:13:22,857
Intercourse specifically late in pregnancy is not harmful.

580
01:13:22,857 --> 01:13:31,670
Sayle and colleagues (2001) reported no increased—and actually a decreased—risk of delivery within 2 weeks of intercourse.

581
01:13:31,670 --> 01:13:44,582
Tan and associates (2007) studied women scheduled for nonurgent labor induction and found that spontaneous labor ensued at equal rates in groups either participating in or abstaining from intercourse.

582
01:13:44,582 --> 01:13:48,295
Oral-vaginal intercourse is occasionally hazardous.

583
01:13:48,295 --> 01:13:58,145
Aronson and Nelson (1967) described a fatal air embolism late in pregnancy as a result of air blown into the vagina during cunnilingus.

584
01:13:58,145 --> 01:14:01,295
Other near-fatal cases have been described .

585
01:14:01,295 --> 01:14:03,570
# Dental Care.

586
01:14:03,570 --> 01:14:10,320
Examination of the teeth is included in the prenatal examination, and good dental hygiene is encouraged.

587
01:14:10,320 --> 01:14:14,857
Indeed, periodontal disease is linked to preterm labor.

588
01:14:14,857 --> 01:14:21,095
Unfortunately, although its treatment improves dental health, it does not prevent preterm birth .

589
01:14:21,095 --> 01:14:24,745
Dental caries are not aggravated by pregnancy.

590
01:14:24,745 --> 01:14:31,370
Importantly, pregnancy is not a contraindication to dental treatment including dental radiographs .

591
01:14:31,370 --> 01:14:33,757
# Immunization.

592
01:14:33,757 --> 01:14:40,145
Current recommendations for immunization during pregnancy are summarized in Table 10-7.

593
01:14:40,145 --> 01:14:52,532
Well-publicized concerns regarding a causal link between childhood exposure to the thimerosal preservative in some vaccines and neuropsychological disorders have led some parents to vaccine prohibition.

594
01:14:52,532 --> 01:14:57,882
Although controversy continues, these associations have been proven groundless.

595
01:14:57,882 --> 01:15:01,920
Thus, many vaccines may be used in pregnancy .

596
01:15:01,920 --> 01:15:14,820
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) stresses the importance of integrating an effective vaccine strategy into the care of both obstetrical and gynecological patients.

597
01:15:14,820 --> 01:15:29,570
The College further emphasizes that information on the safety of vaccines given during pregnancy is subject to change, and recommendations can be found on the CDC website at www.cdc.gov/vaccines.

598
01:15:29,570 --> 01:15:31,945
Table 10-7.

599
01:15:31,945 --> 01:15:48,632
Recommendations for Immunization During Pregnancy and Postpartum Influenza and tetanus–diphtheria–acellular pertussis (Tdap) vaccinations are recommended routinely for all pregnant women (Munoz, 2019; Sperling, 2018b).

600
01:15:48,632 --> 01:15:52,245
Others are recommended for specific indications .

601
01:15:52,245 --> 01:16:00,020
Women who are susceptible to rubella should receive measles, mumps, and rubella (MMR) vaccination postpartum.

602
01:16:00,020 --> 01:16:03,820
This vaccine is contraindicated during pregnancy.

603
01:16:03,820 --> 01:16:05,970
# Caffeine.

604
01:16:05,970 --> 01:16:07,845
Whether adverse

605
01:16:07,996 --> 01:16:13,258
pregnancy outcomes are related to caffeine consumption is somewhat controversial.

606
01:16:13,208 --> 01:16:25,571
As summarized from Chapter 11 (p. 200), heavy intake of coffee each day—approximately five cups or 500 mg of caffeine— slightly raises the miscarriage risk.

607
01:16:25,571 --> 01:16:31,958
Studies of “moderate” intake—less than 200 mg daily—did not find a higher risk.

608
01:16:31,958 --> 01:16:38,271
It is unclear if caffeine consumption is associated with preterm birth or impaired fetal growth.

609
01:16:38,271 --> 01:16:51,096
Clausson and coworkers (2002) found no association between caffeine consumption <500 mg/d and low birthweight, fetal-growth restriction, or preterm delivery.

610
01:16:51,096 --> 01:17:02,796
Bech and associates (2007) randomly assigned more than 1200 pregnant women who drank at least three cups of coffee per day to caffeinated versus decaffeinated coffee.

611
01:17:02,796 --> 01:17:08,121
They found no difference in birthweight or gestational age at delivery between groups.

612
01:17:08,121 --> 01:17:28,308
The CARE Study Group (2008), however, evaluated 2635 low-risk pregnancies and reported a 1.4-fold risk for fetal-growth restriction among those whose daily caffeine consumption was >200 mg/d compared with those who consumed <100 mg/d.

613
01:17:28,308 --> 01:17:48,446
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018g) concludes that moderate consumption of caffeine— less than 200 mg/d—does not appear to be associated with miscarriage or preterm birth, but that the relationship between caffeine consumption and fetal-growth restriction remains unsettled.

614
01:17:48,446 --> 01:17:51,121
# Nausea and Heartburn.

615
01:17:51,121 --> 01:17:56,046
Nausea and vomiting are common complaints during the first half of pregnancy.

616
01:17:56,046 --> 01:18:05,271
These vary in severity and usually commence between the first and second missed menstrual period and continue until 14 to 16 weeks’ gestation.

617
01:18:05,271 --> 01:18:13,571
Although nausea and vomiting tend to be worse in the morning— thus termed morning sickness—both symptoms frequently continue throughout the day.

618
01:18:13,571 --> 01:18:23,333
Lacroix and associates (2000) found that nausea and vomiting were reported by three fourths of pregnant women and lasted an average of 35 days.

619
01:18:23,333 --> 01:18:29,746
Half had relief by 14 weeks’ gestation, and 90 percent by 22 weeks’ gestation.

620
01:18:29,746 --> 01:18:33,846
In 80 percent of these women, nausea lasted all day.

621
01:18:33,846 --> 01:18:41,596
Treatment of pregnancy-associated nausea and vomiting seldom provides complete relief, but symptoms can be minimized.

622
01:18:41,596 --> 01:18:45,383
Eating small meals at frequent intervals is valuable.

623
01:18:45,383 --> 01:18:51,333
One systematic literature search reported that the herbal remedy ginger was likely effective .

624
01:18:51,333 --> 01:19:01,433
Mild symptoms usually respond to vitamin B6 given with doxylamine, but some women require phenothiazine or H1-receptor blocking antiemetics .

625
01:19:01,433 --> 01:19:12,783
In some with hyperemesis gravidarum, vomiting is so severe that dehydration, electrolyte and acid-base disturbances, and starvation ketosis become serious problems .

626
01:19:12,783 --> 01:19:20,096
Heartburn is another common complaint of gravidas and is caused by gastric content reflux into the lower esophagus.

627
01:19:20,096 --> 01:19:32,021
The greater frequency of regurgitation during pregnancy most likely results from upward displacement and compression of the stomach by the uterus, combined with relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter.

628
01:19:32,021 --> 01:19:36,033
Avoiding bending over or lying flat can be preventive.

629
01:19:36,033 --> 01:19:42,821
In most pregnant women, symptoms are mild and relieved by a regimen of more frequent but smaller meals.

630
01:19:42,821 --> 01:19:46,308
Antacids may provide considerable relief .

631
01:19:46,308 --> 01:19:55,108
Specifically, aluminum hydroxide, magnesium trisilicate, or magnesium hydroxide are given alone or in combination.

632
01:19:55,108 --> 01:20:03,146
Management of heartburn or nausea that does not respond to simple measures is discussed in Chapter 57 (p. 1017).

633
01:20:03,146 --> 01:20:05,821
# Pica and Ptyalism.

634
01:20:05,821 --> 01:20:09,308
The craving for strange foods is termed pica.

635
01:20:09,308 --> 01:20:14,946
Worldwide, its prevalence among pregnant women is estimated to be 30 percent .

636
01:20:14,946 --> 01:20:23,371
At times, nonfoods such as ice (pagophagia), starch (amylophagia), or clay (geophagia) may predominate.

637
01:20:23,371 --> 01:20:28,458
This desire is considered by some to be triggered by severe iron deficiency .

638
01:20:28,458 --> 01:20:36,008
Although such cravings usually abate after deficiency correction, not all pregnant women with pica are iron deficient.

639
01:20:36,008 --> 01:20:43,158
Indeed, if strange “foods” dominate the diet, iron deficiency will be aggravated or will develop eventually.

640
01:20:43,158 --> 01:20:52,196
Patel and colleagues (2004) prospectively completed a dietary inventory on more than 3000 women during the second trimester.

641
01:20:52,196 --> 01:20:54,021
The prevalence

642
01:20:54,172 --> 01:20:56,584
of pica was 4 percent.

643
01:20:56,534 --> 01:21:06,459
The most common nonfood items ingested were starch in 64 percent, dirt in 14 percent, sourdough in 9 percent, and ice in 5 percent.

644
01:21:06,459 --> 01:21:12,197
Women during pregnancy are occasionally distressed by profuse salivation—ptyalism.

645
01:21:12,197 --> 01:21:20,209
Although usually unexplained, ptyalism sometimes appears to follow salivary gland stimulation by the ingestion of starch.

646
01:21:20,209 --> 01:21:23,922
It commonly occurs with hyperemesis gravidarum .

647
01:21:23,922 --> 01:21:26,622
# Headache or Backache.

648
01:21:26,622 --> 01:21:29,297
Headaches are common in pregnancy.

649
01:21:29,297 --> 01:21:35,759
At least 5 percent of pregnancies are estimated to be complicated by new-onset or new-type headache .

650
01:21:35,759 --> 01:21:44,234
Acetaminophen is suitable for treatment of most, and an in-depth discussion is found in Chapter 63 (p. 1127).

651
01:21:44,234 --> 01:21:49,572
Low back pain to some extent is reported by nearly 70 percent of gravidas .

652
01:21:49,572 --> 01:21:55,509
Minor degrees follow excessive strain or significant bending, lifting, or walking.

653
01:21:55,509 --> 01:22:04,384
It can be reduced by squatting rather than bending when reaching down, by using a back-support pillow when sitting, and by avoiding high-heeled shoes.

654
01:22:04,384 --> 01:22:12,472
Back pain complaints increase with progressing gestation and are more prevalent in obese women and those with a history of low back pain.

655
01:22:12,472 --> 01:22:19,422
In some cases, troublesome pain may persist for years after the pregnancy (Norén, 2002).

656
01:22:19,422 --> 01:22:27,034
Severe back pain should not be attributed simply to pregnancy until a thorough orthopedic examination has been conducted.

657
01:22:27,034 --> 01:22:37,634
Severe pain has other uncommon causes that include pregnancy-associated osteoporosis, disc disease, vertebral osteoarthritis, or septic arthritis .

658
01:22:37,634 --> 01:22:44,759
More commonly, muscular spasm and tenderness are classified clinically as acute strain or fibrositis.

659
01:22:44,759 --> 01:22:54,559
Although evidence-based clinical research directing care in pregnancy is limited, low back pain usually responds well to analgesics, heat, and rest.

660
01:22:54,559 --> 01:22:57,609
Acetaminophen may be used as needed.

661
01:22:57,609 --> 01:23:05,672
Nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs also may be beneficial but are used only in short courses to avoid fetal effects .

662
01:23:05,672 --> 01:23:11,409
Muscle relaxants that include cyclobenzaprine or baclofen may be added when needed.

663
01:23:11,409 --> 01:23:23,197
Once acute pain is improved, stabilizing and strengthening exercises provided by physical therapy help improve spine and hip stability, which is essential for the increased load of pregnancy.

664
01:23:23,197 --> 01:23:28,722
For some, a support belt that stabilizes the sacroiliac joint may be helpful .

665
01:23:28,722 --> 01:23:31,897
# Varicosities and Hemorrhoids.

666
01:23:31,897 --> 01:23:37,872
Venous leg varicosities have a congenital predisposition and accrue with advancing age.

667
01:23:37,872 --> 01:23:44,772
They can be aggravated by factors that raise lower-extremity venous pressures, such as an enlarging uterus.

668
01:23:44,772 --> 01:23:53,909
Femoral venous pressures in the supine gravida rise from 8 mm Hg in early pregnancy to 24 mm Hg at term.

669
01:23:53,909 --> 01:24:00,797
Thus, leg varicosities typically worsen as pregnancy advances, especially with prolonged standing.

670
01:24:00,797 --> 01:24:09,997
Symptoms vary from cosmetic blemishes and mild discomfort at the end of the day to severe discomfort that requires prolonged rest with feet elevation.

671
01:24:09,997 --> 01:24:16,772
Treatment is generally limited to periodic rest with leg elevation, elastic stockings, or both.

672
01:24:16,772 --> 01:24:27,984
Surgical correction during pregnancy generally is not advised, although rarely the symptoms may be so severe that injection, ligation, or even stripping of the veins is necessary.

673
01:24:27,984 --> 01:24:34,522
Superficial varicosities are a risk factor for deep-vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism .

674
01:24:34,522 --> 01:24:42,234
Vulvar varicosities frequently coexist with leg varicosities, but they may appear without other venous pathology.

675
01:24:42,234 --> 01:24:46,697
Uncommonly, they become massive and almost incapacitating .

676
01:24:46,697 --> 01:24:54,009
If these large varicosities rupture, either spontaneously or at the time of delivery, blood loss can be severe.

677
01:24:54,009 --> 01:25:00,222
Treatment is with specially fitted pantyhose that will also minimize lower extremity varicosities.

678
01:25:00,222 --> 01:25:10,134
With particularly bothersome vulvar varicosities, a foam rubber pad suspended across the vulva by a belt can be used to exert pressure on the dilated veins.

679
01:25:10,134 --> 01:25:17,259
Hemorrhoids are rectal vein varicosities and may first appear during pregnancy as pelvic venous pressures rise.

680
01:25:17,259 --> 01:25:22,059
Commonly, they are recurrences of previously encountered hemorrhoids.

681
01:25:22,059 --> 01:25:25,822
Up to 40 percent of pregnant women develop these .

682
01:25:25,822 --> 01:25:33,272
Pain and swelling usually are relieved by topically applied anesthetics, warm soaks, and stool-softening agents.

683
01:25:33,436 --> 01:25:38,236
With thrombosis of an external hemorrhoid, pain can be considerable.

684
01:25:38,186 --> 01:25:44,486
This may be relieved by incision and removal of the clot following injection of a local anesthetic.

685
01:25:44,486 --> 01:25:47,173
# Sleeping and Fatigue.

686
01:25:47,173 --> 01:25:53,373
Beginning early in pregnancy, many women experience fatigue and need greater amounts of sleep.

687
01:25:53,373 --> 01:26:00,523
The soporific effect of progesterone contributes but may be compounded in the first trimester by nausea and vomiting.

688
01:26:00,523 --> 01:26:07,048
In the latter stages, general discomforts, urinary frequency, and dyspnea can be additive.

689
01:26:07,048 --> 01:26:20,536
Sleep-disordered breathing may be associated with significant morbidities such as hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, stillbirth, and preterm delivery (Brown, 2018; Dominguez, 2018).

690
01:26:20,536 --> 01:26:26,373
Moreover, sleep efficiency appears to progressively diminish as pregnancy advances.

691
01:26:26,373 --> 01:26:41,086
Wilson and associates (2011) performed overnight polysomnography and observed that women in the third trimester had poorer sleep efficiency, more awakenings, and less of both stage 4 (deep) and rapid-eye-movement sleep.

692
01:26:41,086 --> 01:26:46,036
Women in the first trimester also were affected, but to a lesser extent.

693
01:26:46,036 --> 01:26:52,573
Daytime naps and mild sedatives at bedtime such as diphenhydramine (Benadryl) can be helpful.

694
01:26:52,573 --> 01:26:55,198
# Cord Blood Banking.

695
01:26:55,198 --> 01:27:01,611
Cord blood contains hemopoietic stem cells that can be used to treat more than 70 types of diseases.

696
01:27:01,611 --> 01:27:06,786
These include immunological and genetic diseases and some forms of cancer.

697
01:27:06,786 --> 01:27:17,523
Of the two cord blood bank types, public banks promote allogeneic donation, for use by a related or unrelated recipient, similar to blood product donation.

698
01:27:17,523 --> 01:27:25,173
Private banks store stem cells for future autologous use and charge fees for initial processing and annual storage.

699
01:27:25,173 --> 01:27:40,773
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) has concluded that if a woman requests data on umbilical cord banking, information regarding advantages and disadvantages of public versus private banking should be explained.

700
01:27:40,773 --> 01:27:47,461
Some states have passed laws that require physicians to inform patients about cord blood banking options.

701
01:27:47,461 --> 01:27:55,336
Importantly, few transplants have been performed by using cord blood stored in the absence of a known indication in the recipient .

702
01:27:55,336 --> 01:28:01,986
The likelihood that cord blood would be used for the child or family member of the donor couple is considered remote.

703
01:28:01,986 --> 01:28:13,698
Instead, it is recommended that directed donation be considered when an immediate family member carries the diagnosis of a specific condition known to be treatable by hemopoietic transplantation.

